  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., low or zero outcomes) compared to favorable ones. In this case, we analyze the skewness of Lotteries A and B.\n\nLottery A: 2400 Euros with 100% probability. This is a deterministic outcome with no variability. It has no unfavorable outcomes (0 Euros), so it is symmetric or left-skewed (if any), but not right-skewed.\n\nLottery B: 2500 Euros with 33%, 2400 Euros with 66%, 0 Euros with 1%. The unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) occurs with only 1% probability, while favorable outcomes (2400 and 2500) occur with 66% and 33% respectively. The majority of the probability (99%) is assigned to positive outcomes (2400 or 2500), so the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) is very small. Therefore, Lottery B is not right-skewed; in fact, it is slightly left-skewed or nearly symmetric. The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which would have a larger share of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Neither lottery is right-skewed in a meaningful way. Thus, the decision reason is not applicable in a clear way to this decision problem because both lotteries have minimal unfavorable outcomes, and B has only a 1% chance of 0 Euros, which is very small. The reason as stated would not prefer either lottery if both are nearly symmetric or left-skewed. Hence, the reason cannot be meaningfully applied to this decision problem.",
