  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, whether a lottery has a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes (like 0 Euros). In this case, Lottery B has only a 1% chance of 0 Euros, while Lottery A has 0% chance of 0 Euros. Thus, Lottery A has no unfavorable outcomes, and B has a very small one. Therefore, B is not more right-skewed; in fact, it is less right-skewed than a truly right-skewed lottery. The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which would have a larger share of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Here, neither lottery is right-skewed. The individual’s reasoning, however, does not reference skewness at all. Instead, they justify their choice by emphasizing the higher probability of winning (99% in B vs. 100% in A), and the acceptance of a small risk of 0 Euros. This is a different reasoning process — one based on risk tolerance and expected value or utility of non-zero outcomes — not on skewness. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason about skewness. The information considered by the individual (risk of 0 Euros) is not aligned with the skewness criterion. The justification does not reflect the rule of preferring the more right-skewed lottery. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
